# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <time.h>

typedef int64_t time_t;
typedef int32_t clockid_t;

struct timespec {
  time_t tv_sec;
  long tv_nsec;
};

time_t time(time_t *out);
int clock_gettime(clockid_t clock, struct timespec *ts);
int clock_getres(clockid_t clock, struct timespec *res);
int nanosleep(const struct timespec *duration, struct timespec *remaining);
```

# DESCRIPTION

`time` returns the seconds since the Unix epoch by the realtime clock, and stores them in <out> if it is not null.

`clock_gettime` stores the time of <clock> in <ts>.
The clocks, with Linux's numbers, are:

| Clock | Value | Reads |
| `CLOCK_REALTIME` | 0 | UTC, from the clock the process was given |
| `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` | 1 | croi's monotonic clock |
| `CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW` | 4 | the same |
| `CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE` | 6 | the same |
| `CLOCK_BOOTTIME` | 7 | the same |

The realtime clock is set from the machine's real-time clock by the time service.
A process given no such clock reads the monotonic clock as realtime.
Any other <clock>, or a null <ts>, fails with `EINVAL`.

`clock_getres` stores a resolution of 1 nanosecond for each of those clocks in <res>, which may be null.

`nanosleep` sleeps the calling thread for <duration> on the monotonic clock.
A negative number of seconds, or nanoseconds outside 0 to 999999999, fails with `EINVAL`.
Nothing interrupts the sleep, since there are no signals, so <remaining> is never written.
A duration beyond the clock's range sleeps forever.

All return 0 on success (`time` the time) and -1 with `errno` set on error.

